course: everyday
lesson: 380
title: Maddie and Chloe's plaits
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Maddie had fallen in a puddle at break, and her trousers were brown to the knees. In the line Chloe giggled, «Ew, mud monster.» Maddie's face went hot, and before she thought, she grabbed both of Chloe's long plaits and yanked. Chloe yelped and burst into tears. Maddie felt big for about two seconds and then very, very small.
explain: After that Chloe walked the long way round the playground, past the bins, just so she wouldn't pass Maddie. Maddie tried to fix it with a strawberry sweet, but Chloe shook her head and wouldn't take it. Next day Chloe's mum came in to complain that her daughter's head had been sore for days. A sweet can't undo a sore head.
explain: So Maddie wrote a proper sorry note, and it took her three goes: «I pulled your hair because I was embarrassed about my trousers. That wasn't your fault. I'm sorry I hurt you.» Chloe read it and nodded. She told Lily later, «I'm still a bit scared of her. But she's also kind of cool.» Both things were true.
explain: Two years later Chloe wanted to get into the school football team and was hopeless at passing. She asked Maddie for help — and Maddie taught her, every lunchtime for a month. And when Maddie saw her little cousin Evie yanking another girl's hair at a birthday party, she marched over and said, «Stop that. Trust me, she'll remember it for ages.»
fact: Pulling hair really hurts, and the scalp can stay sore for days.
fact: People often lash out when they feel embarrassed, even though the other person isn't to blame.
fact: Being laughed at is upsetting, but it does not make hurting someone all right.
fact: Someone who was hurt often avoids the person who hurt them for a long time.
fact: A small gift is not enough to make up for hurting someone; a real apology is needed.
fact: A good apology says what you did, why it was wrong, and that you are sorry, without excuses.
fact: A person can be scared of someone and admire them at the same time.
fact: Trust can come back slowly, sometimes over years.
fact: Someone who has done a wrong thing and learned from it is often the best person to stop others doing it.
exercise: Why did Maddie pull Chloe's plaits? | She was embarrassed when Chloe laughed at her muddy trousers | Chloe had pulled her hair first, so she was only pulling back | She wanted to find out whether the plaits were real | Chloe asked her to do it as part of a game
exercise: How did Maddie feel right after pulling the plaits? | Big for two seconds, then very small | Proud of herself all day long | Nothing at all, she didn't care | Happy and excited, like after a goal
exercise: Was Chloe's laughing an excuse for pulling her hair? | No, being laughed at doesn't make hurting all right | Yes, because Chloe was the one who started it all | Yes, mud jokes are the very worst kind of joke | Only if Chloe laughed at her more than once
exercise: Why did Chloe walk the long way round the playground? | To avoid passing Maddie | Because the bins smelled nice | She liked a longer walk to class | She was looking for a lost sweet
exercise: Why didn't Chloe take the sweet? | A sweet can't make up for being hurt | She doesn't like strawberry ones at all | She was trying not to eat sweets | She thought the sweet might be poisoned
exercise: Why did Chloe's mum come into school? | Chloe's head had been sore for days | To bring Chloe her lunch from the canteen | To ask Chloe about the football match | To thank the teacher for being so kind
exercise: What made Maddie's sorry note a proper apology? | It said what she did, why, and that she was sorry | It was very long and written on nice paper | It explained that it was really Chloe's fault for laughing | It came with a big bag of sweets
exercise: What did Maddie admit in her note? | That the mud wasn't Chloe's fault | That Chloe was the one who started it | That she didn't really mean to pull so hard | That Chloe was just being a crybaby
exercise: What did Chloe tell Lily about Maddie? | She was still a bit scared but thought Maddie was cool | She hated Maddie and would hate her forever | She wanted to pull Maddie's hair back one day | She'd forgotten all about it long ago
exercise: Can Chloe be scared of Maddie and think she's cool at once? | Yes, people can feel both at the same time | No, you can only ever feel one thing at once | No, so she must be lying about one of them, surely | Only if Maddie says sorry to her all over again
exercise: What happened two years later? | Chloe asked Maddie for help with football | Chloe pulled Maddie's hair back to get even | They never spoke to each other again | Chloe moved away to a different country
exercise: What does Chloe asking for help show? | Trust can come back slowly, even after years | Chloe had forgotten everything that happened | Maddie paid her to ask for help | Chloe was still scared and didn't want to be left out
exercise: Why did Maddie stop her cousin Evie pulling a girl's hair? | She knew how long that hurt is remembered | She wanted to pull Evie's plaits herself, for fun | Evie's mum had paid her to do the plaits | She didn't like parties with lots of people
exercise: What could Maddie have done instead when Chloe giggled? | Said «not funny» and gone to clean up | Pushed Chloe straight into the puddle | Cried and run all the way home | Laughed along and pulled her hair even harder
exercise: What kind of courage did Maddie show with the note? | Admitting she was wrong in writing | Shouting at Chloe's mum at the gate | Pretending that nothing had ever happened | Hiding from Chloe for a whole year at school
exercise: What kind of courage did Chloe show two years later? | Asking for help from someone she'd been scared of | Getting her own back on Maddie at last | Quitting football so she'd never see Maddie | Telling the whole school about the plaits
test: «she laughed at my trousers so i yanked her plaits, she started it». Fair? | No, laughing isn't an excuse for hurting her | Yes, because she was the one who started it | Yes, plaits are made for pulling, everyone knows | Only if her plaits are really long
test: «it's just hair pulling, doesn't even hurt». True? | No, it hurts and the head can be sore for days | Yes, hair can't feel anything, it's not alive | Yes, only babies cry about having their hair pulled | No, but it stops hurting at once
test: «give her a sweet, she'll be fine». Will a sweet fix it? | No, she needs a real sorry, not a gift | Yes, sweets fix anything, they always do | Yes, as long as it's chocolate | Only if it's a really big bag of sweets
test: «why's chloe walking round by the bins every day, weirdo». What's the likely reason? | She's avoiding the girl who hurt her | She likes the smell of the bins | She's lost something round there | She's training for a race at sports day
test: Teacher: «Maddie, write a proper apology». Which is the best one? | «I pulled your hair because I was embarrassed. I'm sorry I hurt you» | «Sorry, but you laughed at me first so it's kind of your fault» | «Sorry if you got upset or whatever» | «Here's a sweet, now we're even, OK?»
test: «chloe's mum is fuming, came in shouting». Why is she so upset? | Her daughter was hurt and sore for days | Chloe lost her lunchbox on the bus | The school ran out of milk that day | Maddie beat Chloe at football at break
test: «chloe says maddie's scary but also kinda cool lol make up ur mind». Can she feel both? | Yes, both feelings can be true together | No, that's impossible, you have to pick one | No, she's just being two-faced about it | Only on different days of the week
test: «chloe asked MADDIE for football help?? after the plaits thing??» What does it show? | Trust can come back slowly after a proper sorry | Chloe has a really bad memory for things | Maddie bullied her into saying it | Chloe secretly wanted to get her back later
test: «my cousin keeps pulling girls' hair at parties, it's just a game». What would Maddie say? | It's not a game, it hurts and they remember it | It's fine to do it if they're little | Pull harder so they learn to keep their hair short | Only pull plaits, never ponytails
test: «I was just embarrassed, that's why». Does that make it OK? | No, it explains it but doesn't make it OK | Yes, embarrassment excuses anything you do | Yes, if you were very, very embarrassed at the time | No, embarrassment isn't a real feeling
exam: Identify the principal constituents of an adequate apology. | A statement of the act, an acknowledgement of its wrongness and an expression of regret | A gift of confectionery of adequate value, presented in person | An account of the other party's provocation and of its effects upon oneself | A promise conditional upon reciprocal apology
exam: Ascertain the most probable reason for which a child who has been injured by a peer subsequently alters her route about the playground. | Avoidance of the peer by whom she was injured | A preference for proximity to refuse receptacles | A requirement imposed by the school authorities | A desire to increase her daily exercise
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises embarrassment as a cause of physical harm inflicted upon another? | It may explain the act but does not excuse it | It constitutes a complete justification | It renders the act harmless | It transfers responsibility to the injured party
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the restoration of trust following an injury and a genuine apology. | It may be restored gradually, sometimes over a period of years | It is restored instantaneously upon the making of any apology | It can never be restored in any circumstances whatsoever | It is restored solely by the giving of gifts
